-- Migration 4: the rate a capture was converted at, kept with the capture.
--
-- Until now a foreign amount had no rate of its own. Every total re-looked the
-- rate up at whatever date the page happened to pass in, and the pages disagreed:
-- the day total used the day being read, the timeline used the trip's first
-- capture, the category totals used the trip's start date. THB 2,555 and INR 50
-- therefore came out as "₹2,605" — one figure added to another with no rate at
-- all, because the lookup had missed and the miss was counted as a zero.
--
-- Two nullable columns fix that at the source:
--
--   rateScaled  the rate in use when the amount was confirmed, scaled by 1e6
--   rateDate    the day that rate was true, so the UI can print "≈ at 20 Nov"
--
-- Both stay NULL for a capture in the trip's own currency (no conversion happens,
-- so there is no rate to keep) and for anything captured before this migration.
-- Rows without a snapshot fall back to the rate table at the capture's own day —
-- not the trip's, not the page's.
--
-- Additive and nullable on purpose: every existing row remains valid, nothing is
-- rewritten, and no data is lost.

ALTER TABLE capture ADD COLUMN rateScaled INTEGER;

ALTER TABLE capture ADD COLUMN rateDate TEXT;
